From a128b30743a7ae81585c61068563e8227163ef48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 15:14:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] control: allocate thrust from GEOMETRY, and make the six
 axes commensurate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implements what duburi_ws asked for in PR #25 §4.2 -- the mixer as a function of
geometry rather than a compiled literal -- for the 5-thruster hull now in
fabrication, without touching the vehicle that flies today.

  FRAME_CLASS = 0  the existing +-1 vectored-8 matrix, bit for bit.  DEFAULT.
  FRAME_CLASS = 1  mongla-5, allocated from measured CAD geometry.
  FRAME_CLASS = 2  vectored-8 as geometry (placeholder arms; measure first).

WHAT A +-1 MATRIX COSTS, measured on this hull rather than argued:

* Its six "axes" are not commensurate. A demand of 1.0 in the yaw column produces
  0.350 N.m/N; a demand of 1.0 in the sway column produces 2.000 N. They differ
  by 5.71x -- exactly the reduced matrix's condition number -- so every per-axis
  gain tuned against them silently absorbed that factor. With each axis
  normalised by its own authority, a demand of 1.0 now means the same thing on
  every axis, and `authority()` states the number.

* It cannot express an off-axis thruster. The axial unit sits 8.06 mm off the Z
  centreline, so surge carries 0.00806 N.m/N of real pitch moment. The old mixer
  left it on the vehicle as an unexplained pitch disturbance for the attitude
  loop -- and then for the CoB auto-trim -- to absorb. Here the vertical pair
  cancels it for 0.0155 of one thruster each.

* It cannot express a dead thruster. Availability is now an input.

Roll is UNACTUATED on this hull, computed from the matrix rather than declared,
and a roll demand is REFUSED and reported rather than rounded to zero.

The mongla-5 reduced matrix is 5x5 with determinant 0.1817 and condition number
5.71: exactly determined, ZERO redundancy. `rebuild()` therefore REFUSES after a
thruster loss and keeps the previous solution, because there is no allocation to
fall back to -- losing one thruster removes an axis. The 8-thruster frame can
re-solve, and the test shows the difference.

ALLOC_PRIO picks how a saturating demand is given up: 0 = uniform (what mix()
does today, so handling is unchanged) or 1 = attitude met in full with
translation taking the headroom that is left. Both are correct and they handle
very differently, so it is a parameter. Per PR #25 §3 the mechanical groups stay
independent -- and for a better reason than before, because the solution operator
is block-diagonal wherever the geometry is. Asserted bit-identical.

GEOMETRY PROVENANCE. Measured 2026-09-25 from the Onshape GLB export's exact
per-primitive accessor bounds. Pair separations are 350.00 and 519.00 mm EXACTLY.
The 346.6 / 520.4 mm figures quoted in PR #25 came from the decimated render mesh
and are RETRACTED; a correction is going on that PR.

MOTOR_DETECT now reads its expected angular response from the geometry, so the
vertical thrusters no longer get |roll| == |pitch| from three equal +-1s.

⛔ The vertical sign convention differs from the legacy matrix (which relies on
"positive command pushes down" to make two negatives into an ascent). Read the
warning at the top of frames.h before any armed dive on FRAME_CLASS != 0.

allocator.cpp includes nothing but <math.h> and <string.h>, so it compiles on a
host: tools/run_native_tests.sh runs 70 checks with no board and no toolchain.
Every asserted number was derived independently from the CAD stations rather than
recorded from this code. Injection-verified -- zeroing the 8.06 mm offset, using
PR #25's retracted 0.1732 m station, removing the roll refusal, disabling the
headroom reservation and replacing the minimum-norm solve each fail it.

